{"timestamp": 1778699984.5883315, "duration_sec": 3.176051616668701, "validator_hotkey": "5HNe4Yx7idMcgrsoPogLVko2AA8Pap33aNyXQhpwBxsS4H4Q", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "mainnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "Most days. And, you know, there are some days where I actually would prefer maybe not to write code. I do love writing code, but some days I don't feel, you know, always completely up to writing code. It's just one of those really dynamic situations."], [0, "And I'm really interested, you know, because we also, on top of the work that we do at Whiteboard, she helps me immensely with Developer T. And she's the one who designed the logo. She's a great designer in general. And she, you know, she runs an Etsy shop and she does all these things on the side as well. We have to always think about balance when it comes to these additional things that we're doing beyond our daily work. And we're always working together. When do we make time to not work?"], [0, "We'll be right back after a quick break for a sponsor."], [0, "Thanks so much to today's sponsor, Codeship. Codeship is a hosted continuous delivery service focusing on speed, security, and customizability. You can set up continuous integration in a matter of seconds and automatically deploy when your tests have passed. Codeship supports your GitHub and Bitbucket projects, and you can get started with their free plan today at codeship.com. Should you decide to go with a premium plan, you can save 20% off of any plan."], [0, "for the next three months by using the code DEVELOPERTEA. Now that code will be in the show notes, so go to codeship.com and use the code DEVELOPERTEA for 20% off today. For fast, secure, and customizable continuous integration, go to codeship.com."], [0, "I know that, Brianna, you run another company in addition to Reactive, correct? I do accounting for, I don't know, six or seven companies in the same space as ours. So it's really fun and interesting, but it is challenging. Sure. Well, in accounting, I feel like, well, I have no business to talk about accounting. I have no idea about anything in the accounting world. Did you go to school for accounting or anything like that? No, actually, I did not. I just kind of"], [0, "taught myself. I was doing books at a company that leased trucks out to semi truck drivers. And it was a very small family affair. And I just kind of had to learn QuickBooks. And so I learned it. And I guess I just made that my job."], [0, "Wow, that is probably an anomaly of a story, especially for accounting. It seems like one of those things that you get put into the pipeline of accountants through the school system, but that wasn't the case. No, no, I came from a totally different perspective."], [0, "school background. I was in nursing school for a while, but that was really hard to keep up with at the time. I was a single parent of two, just all the school and having a job, which I was doing accounting as my job there. I just had to quit and be like, what can I do from home and be able to have a flexible schedule for my kids? And I just go full speed with this accounting thing."], [0, "Well, it sounds like you guys have a huge focus on balance and on, you know, finding the flexibility that you want in life and working from home, deciding, you know, hey, we're going to use this entrepreneurial attitude to gain freedom. Yeah, I mean, I try not to laugh when people say we're trying to find balance because I don't feel like we have balance at all. Right."]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["Coding"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["coding", "software development", "work life balance", "side projects", "graphic design", "etsy shop", "entrepreneurship", "accounting", "quickbooks", "working from home", "flexible schedule", "single parenthood", "continuous integration", "continuous delivery", "software testing", "github", "bitbucket", "career change"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778700366.5948222, "duration_sec": 3.0738837718963623, "validator_hotkey": "5HmkM6X1D3W3CuCSPuHhrbYyZNBy2aGAiZy9NczoJmtY25H7", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "testnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "Good morning, it's February 18th and this is your daily brief in AI. Here's everything you need to know."], [1, "AI takes center stage at BioAsia 2026 in Hyderabad as Asia's leading life sciences and health tech forum opens its 23rd edition with a sharp focus on AI, automation, and data science to accelerate healthcare innovation. More than 175 exhibitors spanning pharmaceuticals, biotechnology, medtech, digital health, and advanced manufacturing showcase a broad tech-bio ecosystem. The event brings researchers, entrepreneurs, investors, and policymakers together to push the tech-bio revolution and address global health challenges. Telangana highlights its strong policies, skilled workforce, and premier educational institutions as key drivers of growth in life sciences. The opening ceremony, led by the State Chief Minister, included the Genome Valley Excellence Award for Cancer Gene Therapy to Professor Bruce L. Levine. Inauguration and awards underscore Telangana's leadership in life sciences and recognize Levine's work in cancer gene therapy. Telangana also unveiled initiatives such as the Young India Skills University and the Telangana School of Life Sciences to build talent in mRNA technology, gene therapy, and AI. Recent moves cited include the Telangana Next Gen Life Sciences Policy, Genome Valley expansion, the launch of OneBio and multiple global capability centers in Hyderabad, The event emphasized ongoing collaborations among research institutions, corporates, startups, MSMEs, and government to create a trusted, future-ready life sciences ecosystem in Telangana. Telangana Rising 2047 envisions making the state a $1 trillion economy by year 2034 and $3 trillion by year 2047 through cross-sector collaboration across research labs, industry, startups, MSMEs, and government. The Telangana Life Sciences Policy 2026-30 promotes cross-border collaboration, international capital, and translation of breakthroughs into healthcare solutions to position Telangana as a global life sciences hub. Plans include shifting from supply-led manufacturing to solution-driven leadership by building talent in mRNA, gene editing, and AI-driven drug discovery, while boosting Hyderabad's role in medical tourism."], [2, "Unilever is partnering with a major cloud provider to run an AI-first digital overhaul, migrating its data and cloud platform to Google Cloud to speed demand generation and deliver actionable insights. The initiative centers on flagship brands Dove, Vaseline, and Hellmann's as consumer journeys become more AI-driven and conversational, aiming to redefine how consumer packaged goods are discovered and shopped. Leaders describe it as a milestone in modernizing legacy systems and boosting agility and consumer engagement in the CPG sector. Market reaction showed UL trading near its 52-week high, with a pre-market around $74.65 and approaching the annual high of $74.97. Unilever reported strong fiscal 2025 results, with total sales up 3.8% to \u20ac50.5 billion across Beauty Wellbeing, personal care, and home care. The collaboration is framed as part of a broader shift toward agentic consumer journeys where AI surfaces influence product discovery and selection. It aims to redefine how brands are found and purchased, aligning with increasingly conversational journeys guided by intelligent digital agents. There is mention of repurposing a former Unilever soap factory in Wales into a modular data center site, signaling broader data infrastructure development alongside the cloud migration. The initiative seeks more personalized consumer engagement and more efficient marketing investments, signaling a move toward AI-driven experiences in the CPG space. Unilever's longstanding relationship with Accenture, renewed in 2024 to scale GenAI, complements Horizon 3 Labs' efforts in Toronto that reportedly runs hundreds of AI applications. The partnership signals a broader industry shift toward AI-enabled transformation in marketing, data infrastructure, and automated business processes within consumer goods, with overhauls of marketing processes to establish new measurement standards, increase conversion rates, and accelerate AI adoption across global."], [3, "Operations."], [4, "The ASM AI Fest 2026 runs through February 28th, showcasing an AI-first mindset across teaching, research, learning methods, and administration to prepare graduates and staff for pervasive AI adoption. The festival features world-class AI workshops that move beyond awareness to practical AI applications, aiming for AI to amplify human capability rather than substitute it. It concludes with a closing ceremony on February 28th, reinforcing ASM's leadership in AI-integrated education across MBA, PGDM, MCA, engineering, undergraduate studies, and school and junior college. A press release includes a photo and logo link and carries an advertorial disclaimer noting the content was provided by PR Newswire and not authored by Business Standard journalists. The initiative is described as one of the region's largest AI-driven academic efforts and positions ASM as a pioneer in AI-integrated education aligned with national AI objectives. As part of the sustained transformation, ASM will form institute-wide AI task forces, establish the ASM AI Center of Excellence, deploy the Navdrishti app for automated student lifecycle management, and introduce AI into PGDM dual specialization programs. The long-term roadmap includes institute-wise AI task forces, a center of excellence for training and research, and the Navdrishti app with AI-enabled placements and broader expansion into academics and finances. Institutional AI roadmap highlights creating task forces, a COE for training and research, and Navdrishti for automated student lifecycle management, with initial AI-enabled placements and later expansion. AI integration spans the entire ASM academic spectrum, from MBA, PGEM, MCA, and engineering to undergraduate and school junior college, preparing learners for a future with pervasive AI adoption. A PGDM dual specialization program with AI integration was launched, signaling program-level AI incorporation across curricula. Program implications include AI-driven analytics for MBA PGDM, ML tools for MCA engineering, AI-influenced entrepreneurship, and data-driven decisions for undergraduates, and foundational AI literacy for younger students. The initiative aligns with India's India AI Mission and Digital India, aiming to position India as a global hub for AI talent and to embed AI across education and administration."], [3, "Ant Group expands its open-source AI family with two trillion-parameter models, Ling-2.5-1T and Ring-2.5-1T, released under open licenses on Hugging Face and ModelScope. The move continues Ling's ecosystem expansion following Ling 2.0 unveiled in late 2025 and positions Ant Group as a key proponent of open AI distribution for researchers and developers. Ling-2.5-1T is described as a flagship general language model focused on efficient reasoning and agent interaction, capable of handling context lengths up to 1 million tokens while using about 5,890 tokens to reach frontier-like results. Ring-2.5-1T is a reasoning-optimized model with a hybrid linear architecture, delivering strong mathematical performance on IMO 2025 and CMO 2025 benchmarks. Both models are part of a coordinated Ling, Ring, and Ming strategy aimed at enterprise AI deployments that combine language understanding, reasoning, and multimodal capabilities for financial services. The open-release approach seeks to accelerate ecosystem adoption by enabling external developers to build domain-specific finance applications, potentially reducing costs and enabling larger-scale deployments. Ant Group frames these releases within a broader fintech trend of integrating large language models and reasoning systems into tasks such as compliance, customer interactions, and risk assessment. Open Access Positions: Ant Group among global contributors to open large-scale AI and supports adapting models for financial domain tasks by external developers. The Ling-2.5-1T release emphasizes efficient reasoning and agent interaction, while Ring-2.5-1T targets higher-order mathematical reasoning and benchmark performance. Overall, the announcements underscore a move toward open, collaborative AI infrastructure designed to support enterprise-grade finance use."], [0, "Cases. Tech is expanding its AI-driven frontline performance ecosystem, anchored by Realskill and Perform, to serve more than 100 enterprise clients and over 25,000 frontline agents worldwide. The goal is to improve associate proficiency and performance, including sales, across global contact centers as part of enterprise adoption. Julie Stone notes that AI alone doesn't drive performance. Translating AI insights into everyday behaviors through realistic practice and timely coaching is essential. The approach links immersive AI-powered practice, performance intelligence, and closed-loop coaching to convert AI insights into daily tasks and repeatable results, eliminating silos between learning, coaching, and execution. The ecosystem centers on RealSkill and Perform to create a continuous skills and behavior framework from onboarding through optimization, with a real-time view of frontline effectiveness. Client examples show tangible outcomes, including a 10% sales increase for an insurance firm and 17-23% NPS boosts for healthcare and telecom clients through data-informed coaching. It positions AI-enabled capabilities as industry agnostic, bridging AI investments with real business outcomes through ongoing enterprise adoption and scale. Measured outcomes include roughly 12% improvement in associate retention, 6 to 8% reductions in average handle time, 100% compliance accuracy within 60 days, a 23% rise in Net Promoter Score, and a 10% lift in sales conversion rates. TTEC highlights a global CX technology and services approach focused on AI-enabled digital CX, spanning AI, analytics, CRM, and omnichannel capabilities, with a continuous feedback loop mapping AI simulations to live coaching skills. The overall message is a cohesive AI deployment that translates automated insights into everyday frontline performance across diverse."], [3, "Industries."], [2, "This has been your Daily Brief in AI."], [1, "To read more about these stories, follow."]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["News & Politics"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["artificial intelligence", "life sciences", "healthcare innovation", "biotechnology", "medtech", "digital health", "gene therapy", "mrna technology", "drug discovery", "cloud migration", "consumer goods", "education technology", "open source models", "large language models", "frontline performance", "fintech", "workforce training", "data centers"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778700471.001275, "duration_sec": 2.286090850830078, "validator_hotkey": "5HmkM6X1D3W3CuCSPuHhrbYyZNBy2aGAiZy9NczoJmtY25H7", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "testnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "So yeah, there's definitely a power to being able to overreact when everyone else is underreacting and also tone down overreactions when everyone else is overreacting when they shouldn't be. So it's, it's, it's been good. We've had a lot of freedom to do that."], [1, "Nice. So why don't we talk, uh, I'm going to cover quickly. We had some changes of course come out. We had Grizzled Company change, which, uh, they lost the real ones to wound. So my opinion on that is, I think when I on a WhatsApp team chat for Ignite, I said, well, that's actually a pretty big nerf. And I think I stand by that because the meta we're in right now, uh, is quite thick and what I would say narrow, right? Like this terminology of wide and narrows is something I use a lot. And narrow means armies are fielding a narrower amount of units, right? They, you know, typically between like 9 and 10 units. And the biggest one there is Necrons, obviously. And reroll 1s to wound is extremely good against Necrons. That's very, very impactful. And, you know, if you've got a Rogal Dorn that wants to shoot a C'tan on an objective, that is something that will happen every single time when you play that matchup. And that's really, really big. The main gun rerolling 1s and then 1 wound is massive. And in addition to that, Rough Riders, uh, which I was playing 3x5 of, that was my main, uh, key unit I think for early skirmishing because they get Lance and then they hit on 2s. So when you charge them, they hit on 2s rolling 1s with +1 to hit, and then they have Strength 9 Lance and they reroll 1s to wound. So if you're just going to try and kill, I don't know, a Rhino or something like that, it's winning on 3s rolling 1s, it's going 8B4 or 5 Marines, for example. It makes them super consistent, and I think that they are the biggest losers of the reroll 1s to wound, because often they only have 1 attack each. So, you know, you roll 5 attacks and you roll 2 1s, right? And then you're like, well, okay, damn. So I think the nerf is\u2014 the nerf is obviously\u2014 I think Grizzle Company is still a fantastic detachment, but I, I don't think\u2014 I think it's definitely the third on the top 3 now in terms of army strength. And I\u2014 that was enough to put me over the line going up to Manchester, saying I'd rather play Ultramarines than Grizzle Company Guard. And I was gonna play\u2014 I was playing Grizzle Company Guard beforehand, so that's what I was practicing for the tournament. And then this change has really put me over the edge. I said, you know what, I don't actually think I've got the strength to get through 4 rounds of probably playing Necron players, so I'm gonna have to go with something that's got to have a little bit more output. Particularly on these close quarters. So I went with Ultramarines, and that was actually a big deciding factor because the two of them were quite close. But I do think Ultramarines pips them now, and I do think it's very obvious that Necrons are the best army in the game right now by a pretty significant margin, I think."], [0, "Oh wow, really interesting."], [1, "Okay, you're really strong, man."], [0, "You mean at the top, top end, or you mean across the board?"], [1, "I think across the board. Doesn't matter what player skill you have, Necrons are the best army in the game. They are extremely easy to play. Yes, yes, you can play, you can play Pantheon of Woe, you can play Starshatter. I think Starshatter is the best take-all-comer list, and you can even play 40 Necron Warriors, which is extremely good right now as well, because the meta is obviously trying to kill C'tan, in my opinion. So yeah, it's, um, it's, it's an interesting one. Necron Masterclass coming out soon, obviously."], [0, "Uh, the\u2014 I've always been a Necron player."], [1, "Yeah, yeah. It's, um, it's a\u2014 yeah, it's a tough meta. I mean, how does Aeldari go into Necrons? Oh, it's just a slog as well, dude."], [0, "I can't believe you just said 40 Warriors and I felt the sweat develop on Aeldari players across the world. Our Discord just went silent for a second."], [1, "It's so hard."]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["Gaming"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["warhammer", "tournament meta", "necrons", "ultramarines", "aeldari", "imperial guard", "detachment rules", "balance changes", "nerf", "reroll mechanics", "unit selection", "close quarters", "competitive gaming", "probability", "list building", "warhammer community", "strategic adaptation", "player skill"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778700721.8286927, "duration_sec": 2.324256658554077, "validator_hotkey": "5HNe4Yx7idMcgrsoPogLVko2AA8Pap33aNyXQhpwBxsS4H4Q", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "mainnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "Yeah, I love that. And don't misunderstand what I'm trying to say right here, but sometimes people think when you hear a sentence like bring more Christ into your life, bring more Jesus, they think bring more religion into their life and religion or church community and all of that stuff, you know, expectations and all that. That's a way of just bringing this being into our lives. Yeah. Oh yeah. That's, that's what we're trying to do. And so we're not\u2014 it's not a list of rules at the center of our worship. It's not a community that we worship. We worship God. Yeah. And he is revealed in Jesus Christ. That's how we come to understand him. And so talk with him, listen to him, bring him into the everyday aspects of our lives. He doesn't control us or dominate us. He invites us, loves us. He forgives us. Ultimately, he redeems us in our small situations and throughout all eternity."], [1, "Yeah, we\u2014 I love the idea that the phrase\u2014 I don't know where I heard it first\u2014 the channels of grace, like just different ways that the influence and power and grace of God can flow into your life. And I just, you know, I think of that when someone goes to church. Church is a channel of grace. I'm going there because my kids and I's relationship is not good. I need to just be in, like, his house. Same, same with, like, the temple, right? I just want\u2014 I remember you teach me one time about just a way to look at the temple sealing as a way that Jenny and I took our marriage to the temple and laid it on the altar and said, Lord, please bless this. Please endow this marriage with your grace and your power, because marriage is hard enough as it is. Like, that's what's going to make it forever is your blessing. Fasting on it. And I just\u2014 I\u2014 that concept of what is it that I can do? Let fasting be a channel of grace. Let sacrifice be a channel of grace. It's not a way of earning. It's a way of inviting more of his power, more Jesus into the situation."], [0, "Amen. That's awesome. Yeah. Can I ask you a fun question as we wrap up today? You know, I know we normally don't do this. Is that okay? That was reviving. Okay, great."], [1, "Are you ready?"], [0, "Have you ever had gumbo before?"], [1, "I grew up in Houston. We're next-door neighbors."], [0, "Is gumbo reviving? Yes."], [1, "Come on. Come on. As long as there's Tony's or Slap Ya Mama on it. There's a spice called Slap Ya Mama. It is so good."], [0, "They got to change the name of that."], [1, "Well, it's because it's the phrase. It's like, this is so good. Makes you want to slap your mama. That's where that came from. So great. But yes, they\u2014 there is some good, good food in Louisiana."]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["Religion & Spirituality"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["christianity", "jesus christ", "worship", "god", "prayer", "grace", "church", "temple", "marriage", "fasting", "sacrifice", "redemption", "spirituality", "relationship counseling", "religious practices", "cajun cuisine", "louisiana", "seasoning"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778700830.4774282, "duration_sec": 1.8737461566925049, "validator_hotkey": "5DkzbwTnocecNohGP4aNVcHZfUgx8uA1MGQ4WB1u3kfi78gE", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "mainnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "I remember when Dara came out because it was when I was working at Ford. So, yeah. That was really cool. So that's why there's other people working on different compilers. Imagine with Wasm, it wouldn't matter what language it's written in. And because Dino, this is like the next one, Dino uses TSC right now, but there's a huge threat on them moving over to this Rust-based one."], [0, "just because it is much more performant to compile your TypeScript before it goes ahead and runs it. And there's an issue here I'll link up. I read about half the thing and somebody went through and described how Dino handles the TypeScript. So it does compile it before it's run, obviously. And then it will recompile it if you're using the watch flag before it runs it again, which makes sense."], [0, "Yeah. Yeah. You have one more here and I have ES build. I wanted to talk about Surcase. I've never heard of Surcase."], [0, "I never heard of it until today. This is another one that came out. It looks to be written in TypeScript itself. Basically, it's like another TypeScript compiler that's apparently faster. I just threw it in here just in case anyone wanted to check it out. It says in the documentation, it says, instead of compiling a large range of JS features to be able to work in Internet Explorer, SirCase assumes you're developing for a recent browser or Node.js version of"], [0, "So it focuses on compiling non-standard language extensions, JSX TypeScript, and Flow. Because of the smaller scope, Circase can get away with architecture that is much more performant, but less extensible and maintainable. I don't know. Like, obviously, there's much smarter people building these things. They do the whole thing where they compare it against the other ones, and it's like a thousand times faster."], [0, "let me tell you when i talk yes build though it is yeah it is okay so that's it i i don't i don't really know whole much about it i don't understand why typescript wouldn't be able to do that if you like pass it what environments you're targeting but obviously it's built for a reason but yes build i've been hearing non-stop about us tell us about that it's a go based compiler does not use babel so basically would take the place of what babel would be in your setup or even"], [0, "And like ours takes the place of where we would use Webpack or whatever on the server side. We're compiling all of our TypeScript with ESBuild now. And out of the box, under the hood, without any modifications, it's supported for TS and TSX files, which means you could just start writing TS or TSX files and ESBuild's going to know what to do with them. But it runs the same way that it would through Babel, isn't it? Just strips all of that TypeScript stuff out. So they actually recommend in their docs to run TSC, no emit."], [0, "In parallel for that type checking, if you want build to break for our server side setup, we only run it in our pull request in our continuous integration. So before pull request, before deploys on merges, those types of things. I should note the Rust based one does not currently do type checking either. So at the time of recording, the only way to get type checking is with the native TSC type script."], [0, "but they are working on implementing it in SWC. And for like most people listening to this, it probably doesn't matter. You just use the thing and it works. And that's cool. But it is interesting, especially because we seem like we're, I think, early days in this stuff. It's interesting to like understand about all the different compilers. So the ES build one looks really, really cool. And you shouldn't really care that it's built in Go other than you know that it's fast. Yeah."], [0, "Because somebody built it in Go. You can install it basically with NPM and then it just exists like any other package that you have. And there's a JavaScript interface for it. So you just write a JavaScript function and it's like it's that or you just use it on the command line. They just added a watch functionality for it, which is great because it didn't have watch before. So now it can watch."]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["Coding"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["typescript", "compiler", "wasm", "dino", "rust", "performance", "transpilation", "esbuild", "go", "babel", "webpack", "type checking", "continuous integration", "command line", "browser compatibility", "javascript ecosystem", "tooling", "watch mode"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778701780.1229713, "duration_sec": 2.1386494636535645, "validator_hotkey": "5C8LULGoFybR7ybg4S8qQkaaowGXFFja4KmMoSqwhxuAsn33", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "testnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "Okay, I think this is only\u2014 not that I've had those experiences, Joe, but this is the times that I frequent the streets which aren't anymore, not too often, these are the stories that they tell. Interesting. Yeah. And these facts, you don't need them for anything but for barbershop talk, but these are the conversations that I have, that I've heard people have."], [1, "It's interesting how different brands market to different people, and I mean, how do they figure it out? Like, what do they work, like, that's where it's evil, right? Like, advertising itself, I don't have a problem with, but I did\u2014 I\u2014 there is something weird about deceptive advertising that's legal, you know."], [0, "Well, they do a history on what people like. I was watching or reading a story about the people that started Forever 21. I forget what the brand was. It was some Koreans from South Korea, not to be confused with Kim Jong-un and those people, but they were tailors or something. They started a small boutique, and what they would do was they would have these clothing, uh, pieces of clothes, and they would really pay particular attention to what colors people like, um, what was, uh, selling the most, whatever. And that's what they buy. And one of the things that made Forever 21 so popular, because they had really inexpensive\u2014 the clothes weren't expensive, but they was turning them over so quick, you know. So people do, do case studies and see what people, uh, attracted to. I know with, um, with Black people, um, you, you put lemon pepper on anything, it's going to go out the roof. You could do lemon pepper chicken wings, lemon pepper french fries, anything lemon pepper, they're gonna go. I don't know who started the whole lemon pepper craze, but you lemon pepper anything, Black people gonna buy it."], [1, "That's interesting, like how white people are associated with very bland foods, you know, macaroni cheese, mashed potatoes, mayonnaise."], [0, "And you know why you're connected with that blandness? Because the way you pronounced it. Macaroni and cheese? You'd never ever say\u2014 if you tell somebody, if you say\u2014 and you would be able to be invited to the cookout, Joe, you know, people like you. If you say, hey guys, I'm coming to the cookout, right, and I'm bringing macaroni and cheese, you're gonna get uninvited to the cookout."], [1, "What? How should I say it?"], [0, "Mac and cheese. Mac and cheese. Yeah, you can't say macaroni and cheese. Nobody ever does that. Okay, you would\u2014 they would look at you as a spy. You would get invited and be invited."], [1, "Well, I'm a different type of white person because I'm Italian and we're associated with spicy food, very strong flavors. Yeah, but it's a different\u2014 like, Italian people don't like bland food."], [0, "They like very spicy, very flavorful food. I'm bringing baked ziti to the barbecue. You'd be like, I'm bringing ziti, right?"], [1, "Yeah, I would say baked ziti. Yeah, because there's different kinds of ziti. You know, there's ziti that you bake and then there's ziti that you just boil and put, you know, marinara sauce on."]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["Comedy"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["advertising", "marketing", "branding", "deceptive tactics", "consumer behavior", "fashion retail", "forever 21", "trend analysis", "cultural stereotypes", "racial identity", "food preferences", "cookout etiquette", "market research", "fast fashion", "data analytics", "italian cuisine", "communication styles", "social norms"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
