{"timestamp": 1778702810.2536368, "duration_sec": 2.1711111068725586, "validator_hotkey": "5HmkM6X1D3W3CuCSPuHhrbYyZNBy2aGAiZy9NczoJmtY25H7", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "testnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "Welcome to the 40K Fireside Podcast, where we discuss everything to do with competitive Warhammer 40K with your hosts Vic Vijay and David Gaylard."], [1, "Hey, welcome back everyone. It is 10K subscriber day for Vic and I, which is crazy. We've been, we've been going hard over what, last, last year. We've really picked it up, obviously, since I've been going full time on this as well. And I think we went from 5K to 10K in one year. So we really want to thank you. If you are not a subscriber, I mean, what are you doing? You're not part of the Fireside family yet. But really, you know, it's been the Discord, I think, is probably been the most special thing for us in some ways, right? The community that we run there. I, you know, I literally check it all day every day, uh, chatting. You know, I'm super active on like Guard and Space Marines right now. Vic is really active on the Aeldari channel, which I think the Aeldari channel has more talk than all the other channels combined."], [0, "You look away, there's 200 messages."], [1, "So, uh, it's beautiful. It's been a beautiful journey. So thank you everyone, uh, for coming along. Vic and I are gonna do potentially like a fun stream game or something like that. Yeah, uh, that'll be cool. And yeah, if you're not liked, you know, or commented or subscribed last time, I'm going to remind you to do that right now. And something else big as well. I had the Ultramarines Masterclass that I've done recently. I think, you know, Vic, the Black Templars Masterclass was like one of the most popular ones actually that we, that we sold. And I had so many people say like, oh, that was, you know, it got me my first GT win or I went 4-1 for the first time. So I did like a 4.5-hour course on Ultramarines, uh, so you can check that out as well. And that's exactly what I'm playing at Manchester as well. I'm a big believer that what I'm playing at tournaments is what I put in these, uh, in these masterclasses. And I top 8'd at LGT last time, so hopefully I'm going to do well with the Ultramarines at Manchester this weekend."], [0, "It suits you as well, that playstyle, doesn't it, Dave? So yeah, no, I'm rooting for you, man. I think that should be a really interesting tournament to see some results on. Obviously the results have been all over the place with the small sample sizes recently, uh, so it'll be to get a bit more juicy data."], [1, "Yeah, you\u2014 I was gonna say, you did a video on Aeldari recently. What was the conclusion of that?"], [0, "Okay, so I did it because I just wanted to see and highlight when armies are broken with statistics, because, um, we had a large period where actually we didn't really have anything that was broken. We had like a flux of strong things, new rules coming out, some things were stronger than others. But, you know, even Ultramarines at its peak was just very strong. We've had much bigger levels of Broken than, than Ultramarines. And then we've got the Necrons update, and that kind of ticks all the boxes for things which can really dominate statistics. Yeah, so I just wanted to highlight kind of the fall of Aeldari and the rise of Necrons over the last 5 or 6 weeks. Very small sample sizes, those statistics are really awful and tough to do in Warhammer 40K. Uh, but Necron's good."], [1, "Necron's good. That was the, that was the summary. But how are Aeldari faring at the moment? I think we had a Sweden, a tournament in Sweden where a player did very well with Aeldari."], [0, "Yeah, so last 6 weeks there's been one tournament win for Aeldari, which has been Axel. Um, and, uh, yeah, with the triple Fire Prisons on GW terrain, that worked out nicely. I didn't even\u2014 I, I didn't even know that they, um, that they used GW terrain over there, but GW terrain it was. So yeah. In, in the video I, I posted, guys, please look past the clickbait title. I couldn't help myself, but the content of the video actually is anything but clickbait. Um, and I, I go through some of the options that you can use on different terrain sets, and it's completely free. I get a lot of messages about the Aeldari Masterclass and like, is it still relevant? It's a little bit out of date now. It is very much about the bigger picture things, so I try to encourage people. It's at a really good price point now because because we've, uh, we've kind of\u2014 we reduced the price after a little bit, right, Dave? Um, so, uh, if you just want to jump in and learn bigger picture stuff, that is still extremely relevant. It's\u2014 I made it so that it would be relevant regardless of balance changes."], [1, "Awesome. And you know, those GW boards can be pretty shooting favorable in a lot of ways, right? And yeah, Triple Fire Prism, I can\u2014 that can do a lot of damage on turn 1. And oftentimes you can shoot deployment zone to deployment zone on turn 1 on GW boards, I've found."]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["Gaming"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["warhammer 40000", "competitive play", "podcast", "subscribers", "discord community", "livestream", "masterclass course", "ultramarines", "black templars", "aeldari", "necrons", "tournament results", "game statistics", "balance changes", "terrain setups", "shooting phase", "video marketing", "online education"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778702999.3427205, "duration_sec": 1.9593737125396729, "validator_hotkey": "5C8LULGoFybR7ybg4S8qQkaaowGXFFja4KmMoSqwhxuAsn33", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "testnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "Even like telling that story because it sounds like bullshit. It really happened that way because then the guard\u2014 I talked to the guard. I'm like, hey, when did the lights go out? Oh, it shook and the lights went out. I'm like, I'm watching that happen."], [1, "So you felt it happen before it happened."], [0, "Some kind of weird way."], [1, "Well, I bet humans have that. Animals definitely have that. They talk about Thailand. How they had that tsunami and all the animals ran up to the highest point of the island. They all just took off. It was like they just knew instinctively."], [0, "I don't know. Nothing like that has ever happened afterwards. But I gotta say, there's been\u2014 I've been lucky over the years."], [1, "Yeah, but you're a dude who's tuned in. You're tuned into your body. You're tuned into your environment. You're not gonna get caught slipping. Like, you probably felt something and your spidey sense went off."], [0, "Yeah, I kind of have been like that growing up. Like, I've been, you know, I've been on my own since I was 14. Been through crazy shit that you normally would see on movies."], [1, "And that's the type of shit that gives you those kind of instincts."], [0, "But yeah, and I was always the one that said, \"Hey, let's leave. Let's get out of here.\" And then, \"Hey, man, there was a shootout that just happened right after you left.\" Or I could detect, like, The predators, you know what I mean?"], [1, "Mm-hmm."]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["Comedy"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["intuition", "instinct", "premonition", "danger awareness", "environmental sensing", "body awareness", "survival", "street smarts", "trauma", "violence", "shootout", "tsunami", "animal behavior", "disaster preparedness", "risk management", "psychology", "self reliance", "decision making"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778703611.9907632, "duration_sec": 2.508650064468384, "validator_hotkey": "5HNe4Yx7idMcgrsoPogLVko2AA8Pap33aNyXQhpwBxsS4H4Q", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "mainnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "So I'm really glad that you mentioned this because cardinality used to keep come up. I mean, I'm sure it still does in the context of Prometheus. And I know that that had memory implications as well as disk implications. It would basically use up more memory, more disk space to store the data."], [0, "Does it affect ArcticDB in the same way when it comes to memory size and disk size? Does ArcticDB use more memory and more disk if there are more labels?"], [0, "So there's at least one fundamental point here that I think I need to point out, which is if you have more data, then you need to pay for it in some way, right? Like there's no such thing as storing data for free, right? Like if we're able to do that, then I think like the fundamentals of computing change. But the characteristics of paying for cardinality are,"], [0, "are dramatically different. In Prometheus, we want to keep series of data alive for as long as possible because that improves compression. And that's ultimately one of the pieces that make Prometheus as efficient as it is. Again, that's why I keep going back to this is a good design for Prometheus because it allows Prometheus to exploit several pieces of that equation to be able to serve"], [0, "things like the super low latency queries like prometheus does in arctic db we're not paying per series we're basically paying per row that we're inserting and the point is we're kind of bringing the cost of inserting a row down so much that we don't care anymore"], [0, "that we how many columns we have in that row so it's where basically our penalty is or our cost is at the row level as opposed to the cardinality level i see i see okay that makes sense because when we used to have lots and lots of labels on metrics in prometheus what used to happen when you would query them you would use a lot of memory"], [0, "So things would take a lot longer. And if you wanted to have them optimized, you would use, I think, more disk space, if I remember correctly, and memory. So I'm wondering, like those ad hoc queries, which you don't know what labels you'll be querying for. So then you just add up"], [0, "I mean, you don't have to declare what the labels are because I think it will also like create different time series. If I remember correctly, this is like all coming back. I haven't used it like in maybe, I don't know, a year now, give or take six months, something like that. And the more labels you'd have, like the more time series you would get. Is that right?"], [0, "That's right. Every unique combination of labels identifies a time series in Prometheus. That's it. And then that is what was resulting in that excessive storage and excessive memory usage, like disk space and memory. And if Parca doesn't do that, that's amazing because that means the cost of a label is much, much lower than it is in Prometheus. As you say, two different systems designed for specific use cases..."], [0, "But ArcticDB seems to have solved this cardinality tackled head on the problem of cardinality, which makes a huge difference. So does that mean that you can store the samples or the profiles that you get with arbitrary labels like customer names or service names or things like that? Because that opens up the world to a host of new possibilities if you do that."]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["Coding"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["cardinality", "prometheus", "arcticdb", "time series", "metrics labels", "label combinations", "memory usage", "disk usage", "data storage", "query performance", "compression", "low latency queries", "observability", "profiling data", "data ingestion", "cost model", "system design", "scalability"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778703761.8975716, "duration_sec": 1.6952474117279053, "validator_hotkey": "5HmkM6X1D3W3CuCSPuHhrbYyZNBy2aGAiZy9NczoJmtY25H7", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "testnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "Good morning, it's February 18th and this is your daily brief in AI. Here's everything you need to know."], [1, "AI takes center stage at BioAsia 2026 in Hyderabad as Asia's leading life sciences and health tech forum opens its 23rd edition with a sharp focus on AI, automation, and data science to accelerate healthcare innovation. More than 175 exhibitors spanning pharmaceuticals, biotechnology, medtech, digital health, and advanced manufacturing showcase a broad tech-bio ecosystem. The event brings researchers, entrepreneurs, investors, and policymakers together to push the tech-bio revolution and address global health challenges. Telangana highlights its strong policies, skilled workforce, and premier educational institutions as key drivers of growth in life sciences. The opening ceremony, led by the State Chief Minister, included the Genome Valley Excellence Award for Cancer Gene Therapy to Professor Bruce L. Levine. Inauguration and awards underscore Telangana's leadership in life sciences and recognize Levine's work in cancer gene therapy. Telangana also unveiled initiatives such as the Young India Skills University and the Telangana School of Life Sciences to build talent in mRNA technology, gene therapy, and AI. Recent moves cited include the Telangana Next Gen Life Sciences Policy, Genome Valley expansion, the launch of OneBio and multiple global capability centers in Hyderabad, The event emphasized ongoing collaborations among research institutions, corporates, startups, MSMEs, and government to create a trusted, future-ready life sciences ecosystem in Telangana. Telangana Rising 2047 envisions making the state a $1 trillion economy by year 2034 and $3 trillion by year 2047 through cross-sector collaboration across research labs, industry, startups, MSMEs, and government. The Telangana Life Sciences Policy 2026-30 promotes cross-border collaboration, international capital, and translation of breakthroughs into healthcare solutions to position Telangana as a global life sciences hub. Plans include shifting from supply-led manufacturing to solution-driven leadership by building talent in mRNA, gene editing, and AI-driven drug discovery, while boosting Hyderabad's role in medical tourism."], [2, "Unilever is partnering with a major cloud provider to run an AI-first digital overhaul, migrating its data and cloud platform to Google Cloud to speed demand generation and deliver actionable insights. The initiative centers on flagship brands Dove, Vaseline, and Hellmann's as consumer journeys become more AI-driven and conversational, aiming to redefine how consumer packaged goods are discovered and shopped. Leaders describe it as a milestone in modernizing legacy systems and boosting agility and consumer engagement in the CPG sector. Market reaction showed UL trading near its 52-week high, with a pre-market around $74.65 and approaching the annual high of $74.97. Unilever reported strong fiscal 2025 results, with total sales up 3.8% to \u20ac50.5 billion across Beauty Wellbeing, personal care, and home care. The collaboration is framed as part of a broader shift toward agentic consumer journeys where AI surfaces influence product discovery and selection. It aims to redefine how brands are found and purchased, aligning with increasingly conversational journeys guided by intelligent digital agents. There is mention of repurposing a former Unilever soap factory in Wales into a modular data center site, signaling broader data infrastructure development alongside the cloud migration. The initiative seeks more personalized consumer engagement and more efficient marketing investments, signaling a move toward AI-driven experiences in the CPG space. Unilever's longstanding relationship with Accenture, renewed in 2024 to scale GenAI, complements Horizon 3 Labs' efforts in Toronto that reportedly runs hundreds of AI applications. The partnership signals a broader industry shift toward AI-enabled transformation in marketing, data infrastructure, and automated business processes within consumer goods, with overhauls of marketing processes to establish new measurement standards, increase conversion rates, and accelerate AI adoption across global."], [3, "Operations."], [4, "The ASM AI Fest 2026 runs through February 28th, showcasing an AI-first mindset across teaching, research, learning methods, and administration to prepare graduates and staff for pervasive AI adoption. The festival features world-class AI workshops that move beyond awareness to practical AI applications, aiming for AI to amplify human capability rather than substitute it. It concludes with a closing ceremony on February 28th, reinforcing ASM's leadership in AI-integrated education across MBA, PGDM, MCA, engineering, undergraduate studies, and school and junior college. A press release includes a photo and logo link and carries an advertorial disclaimer noting the content was provided by PR Newswire and not authored by Business Standard journalists. The initiative is described as one of the region's largest AI-driven academic efforts and positions ASM as a pioneer in AI-integrated education aligned with national AI objectives. As part of the sustained transformation, ASM will form institute-wide AI task forces, establish the ASM AI Center of Excellence, deploy the Navdrishti app for automated student lifecycle management, and introduce AI into PGDM dual specialization programs. The long-term roadmap includes institute-wise AI task forces, a center of excellence for training and research, and the Navdrishti app with AI-enabled placements and broader expansion into academics and finances. Institutional AI roadmap highlights creating task forces, a COE for training and research, and Navdrishti for automated student lifecycle management, with initial AI-enabled placements and later expansion. AI integration spans the entire ASM academic spectrum, from MBA, PGEM, MCA, and engineering to undergraduate and school junior college, preparing learners for a future with pervasive AI adoption. A PGDM dual specialization program with AI integration was launched, signaling program-level AI incorporation across curricula. Program implications include AI-driven analytics for MBA PGDM, ML tools for MCA engineering, AI-influenced entrepreneurship, and data-driven decisions for undergraduates, and foundational AI literacy for younger students. The initiative aligns with India's India AI Mission and Digital India, aiming to position India as a global hub for AI talent and to embed AI across education and administration."], [3, "Ant Group expands its open-source AI family with two trillion-parameter models, Ling-2.5-1T and Ring-2.5-1T, released under open licenses on Hugging Face and ModelScope. The move continues Ling's ecosystem expansion following Ling 2.0 unveiled in late 2025 and positions Ant Group as a key proponent of open AI distribution for researchers and developers. Ling-2.5-1T is described as a flagship general language model focused on efficient reasoning and agent interaction, capable of handling context lengths up to 1 million tokens while using about 5,890 tokens to reach frontier-like results. Ring-2.5-1T is a reasoning-optimized model with a hybrid linear architecture, delivering strong mathematical performance on IMO 2025 and CMO 2025 benchmarks. Both models are part of a coordinated Ling, Ring, and Ming strategy aimed at enterprise AI deployments that combine language understanding, reasoning, and multimodal capabilities for financial services. The open-release approach seeks to accelerate ecosystem adoption by enabling external developers to build domain-specific finance applications, potentially reducing costs and enabling larger-scale deployments. Ant Group frames these releases within a broader fintech trend of integrating large language models and reasoning systems into tasks such as compliance, customer interactions, and risk assessment. Open Access Positions: Ant Group among global contributors to open large-scale AI and supports adapting models for financial domain tasks by external developers. The Ling-2.5-1T release emphasizes efficient reasoning and agent interaction, while Ring-2.5-1T targets higher-order mathematical reasoning and benchmark performance. Overall, the announcements underscore a move toward open, collaborative AI infrastructure designed to support enterprise-grade finance use."], [0, "Cases. Tech is expanding its AI-driven frontline performance ecosystem, anchored by Realskill and Perform, to serve more than 100 enterprise clients and over 25,000 frontline agents worldwide. The goal is to improve associate proficiency and performance, including sales, across global contact centers as part of enterprise adoption. Julie Stone notes that AI alone doesn't drive performance. Translating AI insights into everyday behaviors through realistic practice and timely coaching is essential. The approach links immersive AI-powered practice, performance intelligence, and closed-loop coaching to convert AI insights into daily tasks and repeatable results, eliminating silos between learning, coaching, and execution. The ecosystem centers on RealSkill and Perform to create a continuous skills and behavior framework from onboarding through optimization, with a real-time view of frontline effectiveness. Client examples show tangible outcomes, including a 10% sales increase for an insurance firm and 17-23% NPS boosts for healthcare and telecom clients through data-informed coaching. It positions AI-enabled capabilities as industry agnostic, bridging AI investments with real business outcomes through ongoing enterprise adoption and scale. Measured outcomes include roughly 12% improvement in associate retention, 6 to 8% reductions in average handle time, 100% compliance accuracy within 60 days, a 23% rise in Net Promoter Score, and a 10% lift in sales conversion rates. TTEC highlights a global CX technology and services approach focused on AI-enabled digital CX, spanning AI, analytics, CRM, and omnichannel capabilities, with a continuous feedback loop mapping AI simulations to live coaching skills. The overall message is a cohesive AI deployment that translates automated insights into everyday frontline performance across diverse."], [3, "Industries."], [2, "This has been your Daily Brief in AI."], [1, "To read more about these stories, follow."]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["News & Politics"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["artificial intelligence", "life sciences", "healthcare innovation", "biotechnology", "pharmaceuticals", "digital health", "cloud migration", "consumer packaged goods", "marketing automation", "education technology", "open source models", "enterprise customer experience", "data scienc