{"timestamp": 1778704672.681712, "duration_sec": 2.0258476734161377, "validator_hotkey": "5DkzbwTnocecNohGP4aNVcHZfUgx8uA1MGQ4WB1u3kfi78gE", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "mainnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "Yeah, I've heard many stories of people losing their only backup or they lose their data. And when they go to use their backup, they notice that the backup is not working or it's lost. And if you have like just a hard drive on your house with your backups, if something happens like a flood or a volcano, a hurricane, a robbery,"], [0, "you're probably going to lose your backup as well. And of course, we're not talking theoretically about volcanoes. Right now in Hawaii, there are volcanoes melting houses. So this is, you know, very current. I'm in a very privileged position when it comes to natural disasters, because Brazil is notoriously lacking anything like that. We don't have volcanoes. Unlike Costa Rica."], [0, "Yeah, we don't have any of that terrible stuff you have a lot in the US. But anyway, you can get your house invaded and stolen. And so you have to worry about that no matter what. So how about we break down the different components of backup solutions?"], [0, "Yeah, now that people are scared and they're going to have nightmares about losing their data, let's talk practical. How do you solve that issue? Well, since our audience is mostly coders, let's start with the simplest. Off-site GitHub. You want your code to persist, even if something horrible happens to you. You have a local repo, you have a GitHub repo, and you push."], [0, "Many companies store their own repos in offsite solutions as well. I mean, Git is not limited to GitHub, obviously."], [0, "Yeah, the replication aspect of Git is very nice. For my company, we use our self-hosted GitLab solution, which is in the cloud. And we have several copies of the code in various places. And a thing that I think some developers don't think about is"], [0, "Even if you're working on some side projects on your free time, just create a Git repo and push it somewhere. It doesn't have to be GitHub, it can be another computer, it can be a server you host yourself,"], [0, "or something, but just push the code somewhere because you might lose your stuff. You might do something dumb and want to go back. So having a Git repo, even if it's just"], [0, "A simple personal project is definitely worth it. I don't code without a Git repo, basically. Everything starts with a Git repo already built in, and I commit every now and then and push. And of course, when you're talking any sort of off-site storage, you have to think about privacy. And starting at $7 a month, you can purchase a GitHub membership."], [0, "It goes up from there, but seven is their small plan. And that's for individuals, writers, people who don't do a lot of massive projects with major use on the infrastructure there. But you get an unlimited number of secret repos, private ones that you can do private projects. You can share those around with people. So if you're working with one group,"]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["Coding"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["data backup", "data loss", "offsite storage", "natural disasters", "home burglary", "hard drives", "backup reliability", "code repositories", "git", "github", "gitlab", "cloud hosting", "disaster recovery", "version control", "privacy", "subscription pricing", "software engineering", "risk management"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778704713.5929167, "duration_sec": 1.871701717376709, "validator_hotkey": "5DkzbwTnocecNohGP4aNVcHZfUgx8uA1MGQ4WB1u3kfi78gE", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "mainnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "I don't know if I have the level of trust in their service, nor the happiness where they're costing. How have you found iCloud costs? Yeah, iCloud for storage is unfortunately very expensive. And it's also very integrated with Apple's devices. So it's not very useful if you are outside of the Apple ecosystem."], [0, "But yeah, they are too expensive and I'm hoping that we are going to get some news about that. This WWDC may be higher tiers with lower cost, but who knows. You talked about Amazon and I use a solution that's similar to Amazon."], [0, "But I really like the company and I really like their product. So you've probably heard of Backblaze, right? I, in fact, have just been converted to using Backblaze. That is my thing right now. And it was a journey."], [0, "But I'm not going to talk about Backblaze. I will, eventually. I'm not going to talk about their backup solution. I'm going to talk about Backblaze B2. Have you heard of B2? I have, because you told me about it."], [0, "So as you can tell, I'm a fan of Backblaze B2 and it's a storage solution similar to what Amazon offers. But the thing is, it's very inexpensive. So Backblaze B2 is free for your first 10 gigabytes. And after that, it's 0.005 cents per gigabyte per month."], [0, "And if you compare that to Amazon S3, Amazon S3 is 0.021 per gigabyte per month. So it's 320% more expensive than backwards. So I have to ask."], [0, "Is there traffic costs? Because I know with Amazon, they do charge you quite a bit for all your reads. It's not just your rights. Backblaze charges 0.01%."], [0, "per gigabyte of download and that compared to Amazon which is charging 0.05 so that's a 400% increase if you compare Amazon to Backblaze. So it's still very inexpensive and you can go to their website and use their calculator. I'm using it for"], [0, "temporary file storage, especially when I need to share it with people because I have an app that's called Dropshare and it integrates with Backblaze, so you can just drag some file to the app icon and it's going to upload and give you the link which you can share with your friends."], [0, "And the cool thing about B2 is that they've implemented an API that's equivalent to the Amazon API. So anything that works with Amazon is probably going to work with Backblaze without the developer having to explicitly support it. And I find that backup applications that use third-party applications"]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["Coding"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["icloud", "apple ecosystem", "cloud storage", "pricing", "wwdc", "amazon s3", "backblaze b2", "backup solutions", "bandwidth costs", "file sharing", "dropshare", "api compatibility", "data transfer", "cost comparison", "developer tools", "cloud computing", "competition", "vendor lock in"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
