they're never good."]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["Comedy"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["sex trafficking", "california law", "commercial sex", "force fraud coercion", "coercion definition", "flew out", "flying women", "transactional relationships", "shopping gifts", "legal definitions", "comedians", "comedy rivalry", "entertainment industry", "criminal justice", "consent", "power dynamics", "jealousy", "social norms"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778709802.63585, "duration_sec": 1.6571550369262695, "validator_hotkey": "5HmkM6X1D3W3CuCSPuHhrbYyZNBy2aGAiZy9NczoJmtY25H7", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "testnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "A mob boss?"], [1, "Yeah, he was a dangerous, dangerous guy. He was the guy that that movie that Leonardo DiCaprio starred in with Jack Nicholson. What was that movie, Jamie? Remember that movie that was based on Whitey Bulger? The Departed? Yeah, yeah, yeah. Okay, that was based on Whitey Bulger."], [0, "He was a gangster?"], [1, "Oh, yeah. Terrifying gangster."], [0, "Yeah. What was his demise?"], [1, "He was actually a fucking FBI informant. Not only was he a gangster, he was working with the FBI, and they were letting him get away with shit because he was throwing other people under the bus."], [0, "I think there's a different\u2014 I mean\u2014."], [1, "They wound up catching him in Santa Monica. Well, I've found that\u2014 Hunting Whitey. About that Whitey Bulger won the Mass Millions lottery about that time. Yeah. Yeah, he won the fucking lottery, man. So this is what it says here. What does it say? I think it was more of a scheme than they actually won, but it's always taking money. It's probably a way to like launder money."], [0, "Yeah."], [1, "Oh, 100%. It's a way to launder money. So the way it would work was like, say if you lived in the community and you won the lottery ticket, maybe they would give you money for your lottery ticket, then he would get it, and that way it would show that this is where he got his income from. Like, these guys would all own businesses, right? But the reason why they would own businesses is so they could show why they drive a Cadillac, why they have a mansion, why they have this\u2014 because they have legitimate businesses. But really, these businesses were scams."]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["Comedy"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["organized crime", "mob boss", "gangster", "whitey bulger", "fbi informant", "criminal capture", "santa monica", "money laundering", "lottery scheme", "criminal enterprise", "front businesses", "illicit income", "film adaptation", "law enforcement", "corruption", "financial fraud", "criminal investigation", "social deception"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778710003.3382921, "duration_sec": 8.03246545791626, "validator_hotkey": "5GQyFzvtVMw9X45t6dvUFgR9unFpDCccPPCiAoqJrtPuC8my", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "mainnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "Yeah. So for me, I think I had only stayed at that job for a year when I left and the last six months were like I was counting down the days, to be honest. So we talk a lot in the book about motivation and what are the different ways that you can motivate yourself even when you're not thrilled with your job? Because"], [0, "We're not saying that you should just quit no matter what. And we both did after a time, but I think you can jumpstart your motivation internally. And so I think had I known some of those things earlier on, I could have potentially stuck it out a little bit longer. Yeah."], [0, "So one of the things that we talk about is how can you think about your work as a place of learning? There's something called the IKEA effect, which is that if you put time and effort into something like putting together your IKEA furniture, you are willing to pay more for it."], [0, "than if you were to just purchase identical pre-assembled furniture from ikea and so the more time that we invest in ourselves and learning at work the more we care about it and i just i think i was very much in the sense of i had just graduated school school work was work and i didn't know how to think about oh maybe i could take a class on the side or maybe i could find a mentor within the organization to teach myself how to code or something like that to get there"], [0, "For me, looking back, I would say"], [0, "one thing that that sticks out is again just how anxious i felt and how i wasn't addressing that whatsoever and i think i went into this job with the mentality of i'm an ambitious female and i just want to take over the world and succeed and so my job is my everything right now like in your early 20s you should just be focused on work and building your career and your skill set i did not cultivate a life outside of work as much as i should have"], [0, "And so that made it feel when I was anxious about work, it felt overwhelming. It felt like my whole life was falling apart as opposed to I think if I had, you know, maintained friendships more during that time or had hobbies or outside interests and invested more time in that, it wouldn't have felt so overwhelming when I received critical feedback or maybe when I had a bad day at work."], [0, "So in the book, we really encourage people, you can't be productive if you're only ever working. And research shows that, you know, once you work more than 50 or 60 hours a week, your productivity just starts to dip pretty dramatically. So that's definitely something I wish I had done. And I try to practice now. I try to, on the weekends, you know, set aside time when I just will not be on a screen. And sometimes that's really hard, I think, you know, because we are..."], [0, "accessible 24-7, we often feel like we should be accountable or it's hard to come back and see all these emails and not feel like, oh, I should have responded right away. But it's once you have carved out that time, you realize that the world doesn't end. If you usually respond to an email on a Monday morning as opposed to a Sunday afternoon, it's OK. People also appreciate. I always appreciate when I email someone and I don't hear back until Monday."], [0, "Because it feels nice. It feels like it also gives me space to breathe. So that's I think I think, again, just not not letting your work become your everything is actually the key to being really happy and being able to stay in a job for a longer time. And one last thing I'll say on that is I had a conversation with a friend recently."]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["Coding"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["work motivation", "job satisfaction", "quitting job", "career development", "learning at work", "ikea effect", "self improvement", "workplace anxiety", "work life balance", "productivity", "long work hours", "digital boundaries", "mental health", "mentorship", "coding skills", "friendships", "hobbies", "email etiquette"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778711119.6464548, "duration_sec": 4.528651714324951, "validator_hotkey": "5HmkM6X1D3W3CuCSPuHhrbYyZNBy2aGAiZy9NczoJmtY25H7", "task_type": "named_entities_extraction", "task_raw": {"mode": "testnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "named_entities_extraction", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "document", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "Seattle Council Committee Report\nFinance, Native Communities, and\nTribal Governments Committee\nCouncil Chamber, City Hall\nTuesday, May 5, 2026, 9:30 AM\n600 4th Avenue\nSeattle, WA 98104\nThis meeting also constitutes a meeting of the City Council, provided that the meeting shall be conducted as a\ncommittee meeting under the Council Rules and Procedures, and Council action shall be limited to committee\nbusiness.\nPlease Note: Times listed are estimated\nA. Call To Order\nMeeting Start Time: 9:31 a.m.\nPresiding Officer: Councilmember Strauss\nPresent: 4 - Dan Strauss, Joy Hollingsworth, Robert Kettle,Rob Saka\nLate Arrival: 1 - Maritza Rivera\nB. Approval of the Agenda\nC. Public Comment\nD. Items of Business\n1. Inf 2887 Review of Centennial Accord\nThe Information Item (Inf) was heard in Committee.\n2. CB 121201 An ordinance relating to the 2026 Federation Internationale de Football\nAssociation World Cup; authorizing the Mayor or the Mayor\u2019s designee to\nexecute an agreement between SeattleFWC26 and"]], "participants": null}}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "ner_001", "crc": 1321322, "title": "Extract named entities from documents", "name": "extract_named_entities", "description": "Identifies and extracts named entities from provided documents.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "ner", "prompt_template": "Extract all named entities from the provided document. Include the following entity types: PERSON, ORGANIZATION, LOCATION, and any other relevant entities. Return as comma-delimited list of entities with their types (e.g., 'John Smith (PERSON), ACM Corp (ORGANIZATION)). Only return the entities without any additional commentary.", "output_variable": "extracted_entities", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["seattle city council", "finance native communities and tribal governments committee", "seattle city hall", "councilmember dan strauss", "councilmember joy hollingsworth", "councilmember robert kettle", "councilmember rob saka", "councilmember maritza rivera", "inf 2887", "centennial accord", "cb 121201", "2026 fifa world cup", "mayor of seattle", "seattlefwc26", "600 4th avenue seattle wa 98104"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778711799.2260685, "duration_sec": 1.8813512325286865, "validator_hotkey": "5DkzbwTnocecNohGP4aNVcHZfUgx8uA1MGQ4WB1u3kfi78gE", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "mainnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "In Rails land, it would be like EJB. So you never say like Rails is written in EJB. That would be weird, right? It's just like one part of the overall system. So basically what Elements requires of an engine is that there's like two or three functions of an API like a wrapper that you have to implement. It has to be reactive. It has to be able to take some initial data to render. Those are the basic requirements. Mm-hmm."], [0, "Yeah, I think what you're saying, I mean, like me looking at things out there and the way that people are talking about it, I think you're talking about just the template system, which is literally React. But when people say React, they mean..."], [0, "React, the template system, React hooks, React state management, React forms. When they say React, they mean the framework that is not necessarily the official single repository that is the..."], [0, "react.js repository or whatever it is i actually don't even know what the repository name is but i think i think what you're and that the this dot render i think is very similar to the express version where i'm guessing that you can define a renderer for the whole system or maybe for just a route yeah exactly and then that renderer looks in the path"], [0, "And based on what that renderer is, it's going to, it's still going to accept the same data, but what happens is different. Now, one thing I wasn't clear on while we're back on this was the page that is delivered. If I'm using the React renderer, does that mean that the page that's delivered is hydrated and live? Like I can start clicking around on it. Yeah, absolutely."], [0, "So I don't have to do anything special. Like I render it with elements and I get a page that was rendered with React on the server side, but the JavaScript state is already hooked up on the client side that I can begin using it. Yeah, exactly right. That blows my mind. Yeah."], [0, "Yeah, it was a really hard problem to solve and to do it elegantly. It took a while. And that is one of the requirements of the UI framework that you plug into elements is that it has to be able to support rendering to a string and it also has to be able to support rendering to the DOM. And then ideally, it should also be reactive. So one quick summary point on that, though, is like my..."], [0, "The way I value or look at UI engines is does the UI engine do a good job? And what does it mean to do a good job? One is that the initial render needs to be pretty fast. So obviously the initial render time needs to be linear and the constant factors for the rendering needs to be as low as possible. Then on a re-render, it should also be obviously linear and it should also be as fast as possible. And third, it shouldn't make the user, the developer think too much about performance."], [0, "So the ideal world is that they do the best they can. So if I update the data, that's my job. Now you take that and you render it as fast as you can. Whatever optimizations you need to do, make them. And if as the user, I need to get around that and get lower into the system, I can, but that shouldn't be the default. So I think React does an okay job of that, but there's room for improvement."], [0, "I personally, aesthetically, I like the way Vue.js does the templates. I do like that separation of HTML and JavaScript. Maybe it's an aesthetic preference. I don't know. But I look forward to getting Vue.js baked into here as well. Well, my experience has been back-end developers prefer Vue.js because I think it's more structured and more logical. And front-end developers prefer React. And I think that's because it's more magic."]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["Coding"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["react", "ruby on rails", "api wrapper", "reactive rendering", "server side rendering", "client hydration", "dom rendering", "state management", "react hooks", "vue templates", "performance optimization", "developer experience", "web development", "ui framework", "backend engineering", "frontend engineering", "software architecture", "innovation"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
